anona-compute manages Anona Compute sandboxes through the E2B-compatible API.

Configuration comes from flags, then the environment (ANONA_COMPUTE_API_KEY or
E2B_API_KEY, ANONA_COMPUTE_DOMAIN or E2B_DOMAIN, ANONA_COMPUTE_API_URL or
E2B_API_URL), then the profile in $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/anona-compute/config.toml.
anona-compute doctor checks the whole chain.

JSON output:
  --json (or -o json) prints JSON on stdout. A list command (list, ps,
  template list, config list) prints one array, [] when there is nothing to
  list; other commands print one object per line. Every key is
  snake_case (sandbox_id, template_id, cpu_count, api_url), as in the MCP
  server's tools; keys inside metadata, envs and log fields are yours and are
  printed as given. An error is one object on stderr:
    {"error":{"message":"...","exit_code":N,"status":404,
              "error_code":"...","request_id":"..."}}
  status, error_code and request_id appear when known; a failure with no
  HTTP answer has error_code "network_error" or "timeout".

Exit codes:
  0    success
  1    general error
  2    usage error (bad flag, argument or value)
  3    not signed in, or the credential was refused (HTTP 401/403)
  4    not found (HTTP 404)
  5    plan limit or rate limit (HTTP 429, or error_code plan_limit_exceeded)
  exec and run pass the remote command's exit status through unchanged
  (0-255; 130 after Ctrl-C) and exit 125 when anona-compute itself failed,
  for any of the reasons 1-5 above; their --json error keeps that detailed
  code in "cli_code".

Usage:
  anona-compute [flags]
  anona-compute [command]

Available Commands:
  config      Read and write profile settings
  connect     Open an interactive shell in a sandbox
  create      Create sandboxes
  delete      Delete sandboxes
  desktop     Open a sandbox desktop
  doctor      Check the configuration, credentials and connection to Anona Compute
  exec        Run a command in a sandbox
  fork        Fork a running sandbox into new sandboxes
  help        Help about any command
  idle-pause  Pause a sandbox once nothing has used it for a while
  info        Show one sandbox
  keys        Manage API keys (not available yet)
  limits      Show plan limits and usage (not available yet)
  list        List sandboxes
  login       Sign in through the browser (or save an API key with --with-api-key)
  logout      Sign out: revoke the CLI session on the dashboard and remove local credentials
  logs        Show sandbox logs
  mcp         Run an MCP server so AI agents can use Anona Compute
  output      Print the output of a command started with exec --background
  pause       Pause a sandbox
  preview     Share a sandbox port with a signed preview link
  ps          List the processes running in a sandbox
  push        Push a git working tree into a sandbox (not available yet)
  recover     Recover a sandbox from its last snapshot (not available yet)
  rename      Name a sandbox, or remove its name
  resume      Resume a paused sandbox
  run         Create a sandbox, run one command, then delete the sandbox
  sandbox     Sandbox commands (same as the top-level create, list, info, ...)
  scp         Copy files to or from a sandbox over SSH
  ssh         SSH into a sandbox
  template    Build and manage templates
  timeout     Set how long a sandbox lives from now
  usage       Show usage and cost for a billing period
  vault       Store credentials the sandbox never sees
  version     Print the CLI version
  view        Open a live view of a sandbox
  whoami      Show who the CLI is signed in as

Flags:
      --api-key string             API key (env ANONA_COMPUTE_API_KEY, E2B_API_KEY)
      --api-url string             API URL (env ANONA_COMPUTE_API_URL, E2B_API_URL; default https://api.<E2B_DOMAIN>)
  -h, --help                       help for anona-compute
      --json                       JSON output on stdout (same as -o json)
  -o, --output string              output format: text or json (default "text")
      --profile string             config profile to use (env ANONA_COMPUTE_PROFILE; default "default")
      --request-timeout duration   timeout for each API request (0 = none)
  -v, --verbose                    verbose output on stderr
      --version                    print the CLI version (same as the version command)

Use "anona-compute [command] --help" for more information about a command.
